--TEST--
a closed `Core\Net` handle refuses every member of its class, and closing twice is refused rather than ignored — `rule:core-classes/net-one-api-three-transports` and `rule:errors/on-uncaught-throw`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Net\Listener::accept, Core\Net\Listener::port, Core\Net\Listener::close
// The boundary the happy path never reaches. A socket closes with the request
// that opened it, and a program may close one earlier — after which the key
// its handle carries names an empty slot. Every member of both classes then
// answers the same way, and a `RuntimeError` rather than an `IOError`: nothing
// about the socket went wrong, the program asked a question of something it
// had already given up.
var $door = Core\Net::listen("127.0.0.1", 0);
var $client = Core\Net::connect("127.0.0.1", $door->port(), 5s);
var $served = $door->accept(5s);

$served->close();
$client->close();
$door->close();

try { $served->read(4, 1s); } catch (RuntimeError $e) { echo $e->message, "\n"; }
try { $served->write("x" as bytes, 1s); } catch (RuntimeError $e) { echo $e->message, "\n"; }
try { $client->close(); } catch (RuntimeError $e) { echo $e->message, "\n"; }
try { $door->accept(1s); } catch (RuntimeError $e) { echo $e->message, "\n"; }
try { $door->port(); } catch (RuntimeError $e) { echo $e->message, "\n"; }

// A key is never reused within a request, so a second `close` can only be the
// same handle twice — which is a mistake worth naming rather than a no-op.
try { $door->close(); } catch (RuntimeError $e) { echo $e->message, "\n"; }
--FILE nvs.toml--
# The same grant the round-trip case writes, because this case has to *open*
# the sockets before it can close them.
[capabilities.net]
listen = true
connect = ["127.0.0.1"]
internal = ["127.0.0.1"]
--EXPECT--
Core\Net\Stream::read: this handle is closed
Core\Net\Stream::write: this handle is closed
Core\Net\Stream::close: this handle is closed
Core\Net\Listener::accept: this handle is closed
Core\Net\Listener::port: this handle is closed
Core\Net\Listener::close: this handle is closed
